so we have four micronus and we have a  question already up here in the back  right hi thank you madam secretary my  name is cheyenne ellis and i'm an  american here at living in copenhagen  prior to moving to Denmark I worked in  Washington DC at the Department of State  along with suit Ambassador Susan Jacobs  on parental child abduction cases  however today my question is on gender  imbalance in the workplace recently the  EU has proposed to initiate mandatory  quotas to ensure more women are  represented in top management positions  in European businesses do you feel a  quota system is necessary to increase  more female CEOs or do you feel like  this is a self-regulating issue which  needs no policy intervention well  Cheyenne that's a question I've wrestled  with for a long time and I you know I  don't think that there is a right answer  to that question because there does seem  to be a glass ceiling in nearly every  society not all of them I think that you  know you now have a woman Prime Minister  you have a woman head of state be going  to Norway and Sweden and Finland all of  whom have had women in the very top  positions in government but even in  those four countries there's not that  level of representation on a sustained  basis in the corporate world so is there  some intervention that could encourage  businesses to look more broadly to  recruit women I'm sure that there could  be whether it's quotas or whether quotas  are the right answer I will leave that  debate to the Europeans to decide  whether it's right for them but  certainly it's been our experience that  it still is more difficult for women to  make it to the top in either politics or  business in our country the United  States would never go for quotas but I  think there has to be  recognition that in the absence of a  much more self-conscious outreach for  women to be on corporate boards in the  pipeline for corporate leadership  positions it's not likely to change so  I'm hoping that as we experiment with  this in different parts of the world we  will learn more and if the EU adopts  quotas I will be very interested in  seeing how that works or whether it is  unsuccessful because people just  flat-out resistant I hope though that we  can finally get over what has been a  historic gender imbalance in both  politics and business because I think in  the 21st century women's empowerment is  one of the most important goals for the  world to aim toward thank you for that  answer Madam Secretary let's go to the  front left Ingrid please hi my name is  mess I wanted to ask you for a student  of international affairs and global  politics where do you believe a future  in in these areas live for us I put it  in the UN on the national stage or maybe  in the NGOs and guess the question is  also which institutions do you think the  future of political or global politics  will be shaped well I'm glad you're  interested in these areas and I think  they're our futures in all three of what  you said I think the multilateral  organizations are in great need of  reform and young people need to be  committed not only to a career but to a  reform agenda within the institutions  because I think that they haven't kept  up with the times I don't think that  they operate as efficiently as they  could they get too bureaucratic to  weighted down by internal process  instead of external results I think NGO  is our a lean mean way often of  intervening of making your voices heard  one of my goals is to promote civil  society in places that have never known  civil society and to make it legitimate  to convince governments that having  people who advocate for the environment  or for children with disabilities or for  the arts or for electoral reform is an  essential part of the democratic process  and then government obviously I believe  strongly that if we're lucky enough to  live in a vibrant democratic society you  can either be totally apathetic which  then leaves the decision-making to  people who may disagree with you  completely or you can be an active  citizen participate participant in  politics or you can even go so far as  being active in a political party and  running for office it's hard I'm not  going to sugarcoat it it's not easy  being in politics anywhere in the world  today in any democracy but I certainly  hope that a lot of young people like  yourself we'll look at that third  alternative as much as the first to  Madam Secretary if you were 18 again  living in the world as of today with all  the economic instability and uncertainty  what would you choose to do for a living  that's a great question well you know I  became a lawyer and I practice law and I  did a lot of work on behalf of defending  children abused and neglected children  and working for legislative change on  behalf of children and I always find  myself attracted to trying to work on  behalf of those who are marginalized or  left out so I would probably do the same  thing whether i mean i've started NGOs  i've served on the boards of large NGOs  and help to direct them and you know I  think there will always be a voice even  in privileged societies like ours for  those  who are voiceless and so I I like  feeling that maybe I can you know help  make a difference in someone's life and  then the work around the world is a full  agenda particularly on behalf of women  and children so I think I'd probably be  doing something in that area which is  what I was doing before I got into  politics let's go to Row 3 here please  first of all it's a huge honor Madam  Secretary my name is Anna I'm a PhD  fellow in American Studies and I have a  question about Syria of course one of  the reasons why the operation in Libya  went so we'll was because you had the  support of other Arab countries which  role what role do you think arab  countries will play in the solution of  the Syrian problem and also you say that  the Russians are the main problem why  there's not a sort of solution in the  international community at this point  are there other sort of regional and  national differences between Syria and  Libya that makes it difficult to to  intervene that's a great great question  there are certainly significant  differences Syria is much larger it's a  much more diverse society so that  different groups within Syria are  worried about what comes after Assad and  are not unified in the way that Libya  became there is a professional military  there was not in Libya Gaddafi relied on  a very small group of military personnel  augmented by mercenaries there was a  opposition in Libya that did represent  the country that's not been yet possible  in Syria we've all been working to try  to promote that there was a a safe haven  that could be operated out of that  Benghazi became and then you could move  west the air defenses in Syria are  significantly tougher than Libya the  Arab League called for action by the  Security Council the Arab League has  supported the Kofi Annan mission they  haven't been united to call for military  action yet and most importantly the  Security Council in the case of Libya  was willing to act and then NATO could  put together a coalition that was  augmented by Arab countries willing to  fly the no-fly zone even carry out  strikes so really those conditions do  not exist with respect to Syria as of  yet there's also a lot of regional  difficulty or complexity that has to be  dealt with I mean you know Jordan is  right on the border of Syria you know  they have to worry about their own  territorial integrity and safety Turkey  has that long border they worry about  whether they make themselves more  vulnerable to the Kurdish terrorist  threat that they are so focused on you  go down the line it's quite a difficult  set of factors to balance its next door  to Lebanon which as you know fought a  brutal civil war all those years and the  demographics of the population are not  so dissimilar except in terms of numbers  but the basic demographics are quite  similar between the two countries we've  already seen the conflict wash over and  a Lebanon and a lot of people are trying  to figure out what could be an effective  intervention that wouldn't cause more  death and suffering and in Libya partly  because it was a small population in a  asked expanse much of it not  particularly populated there was a  theater for intervention that was quite  successful in avoiding civilian  casualties that seems much more  difficult if not impossible now we're  thinking about all of this there's all  kinds of both civilian and humanitarian  and military planning going on but the  factors are just not there you ask very  good question guys thanks for doing my  job so well let's go to the far back  left hi my name is Heidi of study  information management and I have  recently applied for an internship at  MoMA in New York and that made me  research a bit about the American  education system we have a really good  one here but don't you find that you  leave big part of the American youth on  the ground because they can't afford or  their parents can't afford to give them  an education I think it's one of our  biggest domestic challenges now  historically our public education system  worked well enough for the vast majority  of Americans and a relatively small  percentage of Americans went to college  but by the end of the 20th century and  certainly now into the 21st century the  disparities in education between our  most privileged young people and our  poorest has gotten much broader and the  cost of going to college has skyrocketed  so we have increasing numbers of young  people who are coming out of college  with huge debt and it's really  regrettable and there there has to be a  reckoning about this because we're not  in my view providing the best education  we should for children who are  not in the best of circumstances and we  make a post-high school education very  expensive and we don't have enough of  the right sort of skills training  because one of the great ironies in our  current economic situation is you can  see job postings for thousands of jobs  but we don't have the people in the  places with the skills to take the jobs  so we have to close that gap and it's  it's something that I care deeply about  I know President Obama does as well and  we're going to see what more we can do  from the federal government although  final thing I would say on this is you  know much of education starts in the  family and requires a combination of  encouragement and discipline that  families have to provide and then our  education system in public schools is  run by local and state governments so  our complicated federal system makes  change more challenging but we have to  figure out how we're going to do it okay  Ingrid let's go to the front row middle  please be careful not to break a leg  please hi my name is Tina and I studied  law at copenhagen university my question  is about the International Criminal  Court your husband been told till he  signed the Rome Statute in two thousand  and what the Bush administration later  unsigned it you seem to have a rather  positive approach to the ICC so my  question is whether you plan to sign re  sign and ratify the Rome Statute during  your time as Secretary of State well I  do think that the ICC is an important  tribunal and I thank you for noticing  that we have in the last three and a  half years increased our  involvement with the ICC the head of our  our legal department at the State  Department is a renowned international  lawyer the former dean of the Yale Law  School and we have promoted more  exchanges more discussions but at this  point we we know that there would be no  appetite in our Congress under current  circumstances to ratify America's  membership so short of that we're going  to continue to do what we can to support  the important work that the ICC does  Madame secretary i would like to squeeze  in a little question that we also got  from a lot of our viewers we asked on  Facebook that they could they could ask  you questions and and thousands of  people around the globe especially women  and see you as a role model because you  have had a remarkable career you're the  first lady you've been the senator for  New York and now you're the Secretary of  State and can you tell us a little thing  about how has this journey been for you  personally well personally it's been a  surprise because when I was sitting  where you would be sitting I never  imagined that I would marry someone  who'd become a president or that I would  be a senator from New York or that I  would be a secretary of state so my my  journey has been not only surprising but  you know very gratifying to me and  people ask me all the time if I have any  advice and it's hard to put yourself in  anyone else's shoes but I think for both  young men and young women to go back to  the previous question education remains  such a passport to any option that you  might decide to choose and it doesn't  always have to be conventional education  people i know very successful people  Bill Gates obviously dropped out of  Harvard but he was always  learning and he was always alert and he  was always taking in what he was  interested in and was able to channel  his passion into creative innovation so  staying involved and and aware of what's  going on around you I also always hope  that people develop some sense of social  responsibility that if you're fortunate  enough to be educated healthy living in  a democracy having more control over  your own life although it probably  doesn't seem like it from time to time  then the vast majority of people who've  ever lived in the history of the world  have had then what can you do to give  back as well as get ahead and for young  women I think that the historic  questions remain I mean how can you have  a life and make a living how can you  have relationships how 